# Converting `std::string` to `std::vector` in C++

There are several ways to convert a `std::string` to a `std::vector` depending on what you want to store in the vector:

## 1. Vector of characters (most common case)

```cpp
#include <string>
#include <vector>

std::string str = "Hello";
std::vector<char> vec(str.begin(), str.end());
```

## 2. Vector of bytes (unsigned char)

```cpp
std::string str = "Hello";
std::vector<unsigned char> vec(str.begin(), str.end());
```

## 3. Vector of integers (ASCII values)

```cpp
std::string str = "Hello";
std::vector<int> vec(str.begin(), str.end());
```

## 4. Using range constructor (C++11 and later)

```cpp
std::string str = "Hello";
std::vector<char> vec{str.begin(), str.end()};
```

## 5. For UTF-8 strings (if you need Unicode code points)

```cpp
#include <codecvt>
#include <locale>

std::string str = "Hello";
std::wstring_convert<std::codecvt_utf8<char32_t>, char32_t> converter;
std::u32string utf32 = converter.from_bytes(str);
std::vector<char32_t> vec(utf32.begin(), utf32.end());
```

## 6. Using std::copy

```cpp
std::string str = "Hello";
std::vector<char> vec(str.size());
std::copy(str.begin(), str.end(), vec.begin());
```

The first method (vector of characters) is the most common and straightforward approach for most use cases.

